RULES COVERAGE. The rules cover every situation:
- validity of bids (eligibility, minimum standing+1, budget cap);
- tie handling at the top (no change);
- no-bid handling;
- eligibility (made a valid bid or is leader);
- unsold items (leader 0, so last_price is 0);
- negative scores;
- a budget of 0 (only pass is possible);
- final ties (a draw).

FINITE AND ARITHMETIC. The game has 6 rounds of 3 turns each. All quantities are integers the game keeps: budget, score, spent, values, eligible, standing_bid, leader and last_price.

LIMITS. Every stated figure is within the platform limits:
| Item | Stated | Limit |
|---|---|---|
| steps_per_round | 15 | 20 |
| choice_steps_per_round | 4 (1 talk + 3 bid polls) | 4 |
| attributes | 11 | 20 |
| hidden_elements | 1 (values list) | 3 |
| random_draws_per_round | 1 (one dealing step, guarded so it never overwrites) | 1 |
| rounds | 6 | 40 |
All choices are simultaneous polls, as required. No board, clock, judge, trading or binding agreement is needed.

TALK. With 3-6 players, interests conflict: everyone wants items cheaply, and claims like 'I'll take item 3, you take 4' are tempting but not binding. Following a proposal can cost a player, because deferring to a bluffer means losing a high-value item. Talk is simultaneous and gives players something to reason about, since claims can be checked against later bids. Talk is therefore appropriate.

MINOR REDUNDANCY. R7(c) duplicates the R6 poll range. This is harmless.
